1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,078
An engineer pushes a commit, which
sends it to the team's shared server.

2
00:00:09,118 --> 00:00:14,208
Then they notice a typo in the message,
fix it with git commit --amend, and push

3
00:00:14,248 --> 00:00:19,467
again. The push is rejected. Git prints a
hint: use git pull before pushing again.

4
00:00:19,807 --> 00:00:23,818
They pull, which fetches the
server's commits and merges them in.

5
00:00:23,858 --> 00:00:28,741
Now the history has the old commit, the
new commit, and a merge of the two: two

6
00:00:28,781 --> 00:00:32,481
versions of one change, joined
together, on a shared branch.

7
00:00:32,521 --> 00:00:35,098
Was pulling the right move? Say yes or no.

8
00:00:37,438 --> 00:00:42,028
No. The hint was the wrong advice for this
situation, and following it made things

9
00:00:42,068 --> 00:00:45,754
worse. To see why, you need one
fact: amend doesn't edit a commit.

10
00:00:45,794 --> 00:00:50,270
It creates a sibling, and moves your branch
to it. Today is about that fact, and

11
00:00:50,310 --> 00:00:54,279
about what makes a commit good enough
that you rarely need to amend it.

12
00:00:54,319 --> 00:00:57,666
And you'll leave with the two
right moves for that engineer.

13
00:00:59,506 --> 00:01:03,310
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:07,470
This video closes the block on
commits with five practical topics.

15
00:01:07,810 --> 00:01:12,051
git commit --amend: what it creates,
and where the old commit goes.

16
00:01:12,091 --> 00:01:17,162
Empty commits. Trailers: structured lines
at the end of a message that tools can

17
00:01:17,202 --> 00:01:20,549
parse. How Git reads a
message, and how to write one.

18
00:01:20,589 --> 00:01:24,383
And atomic commits: commits that
can be reverted one by one.

19
00:01:24,423 --> 00:01:29,750
At the end, a short look at where a signature
is stored, as a preview of the part on

20
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:30,325
security.

21
00:01:32,165 --> 00:01:33,743
Here's what you'll walk away with.

22
00:01:34,083 --> 00:01:35,496
After this video you can:

23
00:01:35,836 --> 00:01:40,656
Show that git commit --amend creates
a new commit, and find the old one.

24
00:01:40,996 --> 00:01:44,320
Predict what happens when an amended
commit had already been pushed.

25
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:47,453
Write trailers that Git
recognizes, and query them.

26
00:01:47,793 --> 00:01:52,558
Split work into atomic commits, and justify
the split by what a revert would do.

27
00:01:52,898 --> 00:01:55,605
Say where a signature is
stored in a commit object.

28
00:01:57,445 --> 00:02:02,609
Amend. In one sentence: git commit --amend
writes a new commit that takes the place

29
00:02:02,649 --> 00:02:07,374
of the current one: same parent, new
tree and message as you choose, new ID.

30
00:02:07,414 --> 00:02:08,377
It is 🟡 CAUTION.

31
00:02:08,717 --> 00:02:14,100
Quick quiz, three options. What happens to
the old commit: is it edited, deleted, or

32
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:16,359
left where it was? Say your answer.

33
00:02:21,199 --> 00:02:25,805
Left where it was. The manual's description
is "Replace the tip of the current

34
00:02:25,845 --> 00:02:30,452
branch by creating a new commit", and
"The new commit has the same parents and

35
00:02:30,492 --> 00:02:34,919
author as the current one". The old
commit is neither modified nor deleted.

36
00:02:34,959 --> 00:02:39,386
The branch stops pointing at it, and
only reflog entries still refer to it.

37
00:02:39,426 --> 00:02:43,198
The reflog is Git's local journal
of where a branch has pointed.

38
00:02:43,538 --> 00:02:48,836
How long the old commit survives. By default,
a reflog entry that is not reachable

39
00:02:48,876 --> 00:02:53,587
from the current tip expires after 30
days, and any other entry after 90.

40
00:02:53,627 --> 00:02:59,054
An object that no ref and no reflog entry
refers to is pruned by maintenance once it

41
00:02:59,094 --> 00:03:03,936
is more than two weeks old. A ref is a
name for a commit, such as a branch.

42
00:03:03,976 --> 00:03:09,273
The lab configuration sets the two reflog
values to never, so replays don't depend

43
00:03:09,313 --> 00:03:10,315
on the calendar.

44
00:03:10,655 --> 00:03:16,053
When to amend, and when not. Amend freely
while a commit exists only in your

45
00:03:16,093 --> 00:03:21,634
repository. --no-edit keeps the message,
--only leaves staged changes out, and

46
00:03:21,674 --> 00:03:27,502
--reset-author repairs a wrong identity.
Remember that everything staged goes into

47
00:03:27,542 --> 00:03:29,720
an amend unless you say --only.

48
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:34,759
Don't amend what others may have: on a
shared branch an amend produces a divergence

49
00:03:34,799 --> 00:03:39,384
for every colleague. And an amend doesn't
delete anything: a secret committed and

50
00:03:39,424 --> 00:03:44,066
then amended away is still in the old
commit, in your reflog, and on the server if

51
00:03:44,106 --> 00:03:45,950
it was pushed. Rotate the secret.

52
00:03:46,290 --> 00:03:51,438
Empty commits. An empty commit has the same
tree as its parent: a point in history

53
00:03:51,478 --> 00:03:55,550
with a message and no change.
Without --allow-empty, Git refuses.

54
00:03:55,590 --> 00:04:00,801
The manual says the option "is primarily
for use by foreign SCM interface scripts".

55
00:04:00,841 --> 00:04:05,609
Teams use it to start a push-triggered
pipeline, an automated build, without

56
00:04:05,649 --> 00:04:10,607
touching a file. Two cautions from the
textbook. A pipeline filtered by changed

57
00:04:10,647 --> 00:04:14,656
paths has no path to match, so an
empty commit may not start it.

58
00:04:14,696 --> 00:04:18,515
And the commit stays in history.
Empty commits are permanent.

59
00:04:18,855 --> 00:04:23,893
Trailers. In one sentence: a trailer is a
Key: value line in the last paragraph of a

60
00:04:23,933 --> 00:04:28,851
commit message: ordinary text, placed where
Git and other tools can find and parse

61
00:04:28,891 --> 00:04:33,747
it. git commit -s adds a Signed-off-by
trailer with the committer's identity, and

62
00:04:33,787 --> 00:04:38,765
--trailer adds any other. The trailers are
part of the message, so they are part of

63
00:04:38,805 --> 00:04:40,700
the commit object and of its ID.

64
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:45,822
The rules. Git recognises a trailer block
only at the end of the message, after an

65
00:04:45,862 --> 00:04:50,703
empty line. Every line of the block must
be a trailer, unless at least a quarter of

66
00:04:50,743 --> 00:04:55,113
the lines are, and one of them has a
key that Git generates itself, such as

67
00:04:55,153 --> 00:04:58,289
Signed-off-by, or a key
defined in your configuration.

68
00:04:58,629 --> 00:05:03,432
What they mean. Signed-off-by: a statement
defined by the project, commonly the

69
00:05:03,472 --> 00:05:07,969
Developer Certificate of Origin. It's
text, not a cryptographic signature.

70
00:05:08,009 --> 00:05:12,261
Co-authored-by: credit for a further
author. Git only stores the line.

71
00:05:12,301 --> 00:05:16,859
Others such as Reviewed-by, Refs and
Fixes mean whatever your team defines.

72
00:05:17,199 --> 00:05:23,035
Version notes from the textbook. git
commit --trailer exists since Git 2.32.

73
00:05:23,075 --> 00:05:29,298
git shortlog --group=trailer:<key> since
Git 2.29. And up to Git 2.55 the trailer

74
00:05:29,338 --> 00:05:33,937
parser can take a line that
begins with a URL for a trailer.

75
00:05:33,977 --> 00:05:39,813
Git 2.56 no longer does. That last one
is "added in Git 2.56, not run here".

76
00:05:40,153 --> 00:05:45,155
How Git reads a message. Two rules matter.
First, the title is the text up to the

77
00:05:45,195 --> 00:05:50,384
first empty line, not the first line. Without
the empty line, your body becomes part

78
00:05:50,424 --> 00:05:55,365
of the title. Second, when a message passes
through the editor, lines that begin

79
00:05:55,405 --> 00:05:59,287
with # are comments and are
removed. Don't start a line with #.

80
00:05:59,627 --> 00:06:00,980
Craft, with the reasons.

81
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:06,773
A title of about 50 characters without a
full stop: because, in the manual's words,

82
00:06:06,813 --> 00:06:11,934
"that title is used throughout Git":
one-line logs, shortlog, branch listings,

83
00:06:11,974 --> 00:06:16,831
reflogs, mail subjects and patch file
names. An empty line, then the body.

84
00:06:17,171 --> 00:06:22,029
Imperative mood, "Retry judge calls", not
"Retried": the Git project asks for it,

85
00:06:22,069 --> 00:06:26,987
"as if you are giving orders to the codebase".
The body states the problem and why

86
00:06:27,027 --> 00:06:31,401
this solution: the diff shows what
changed, and nothing else records why.

87
00:06:31,441 --> 00:06:34,908
Lines of at most about 72
characters: Git doesn't re-wrap.

88
00:06:34,948 --> 00:06:37,267
And machine-readable facts as trailers.

89
00:06:37,607 --> 00:06:41,871
Team conventions such as Conventional
Commits add a pattern to the title.

90
00:06:41,911 --> 00:06:46,528
They're agreements on top of Git, enforced
by hooks or CI if at all, meaning by

91
00:06:46,568 --> 00:06:49,240
scripts on your machine
or checks on a server.

92
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:54,240
Atomic commits. An atomic commit contains
one logical change, complete enough that

93
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:58,596
the project still builds and passes its
tests. The Git project's rule: "Make

94
00:06:58,636 --> 00:07:03,411
separate commits for logically separate
changes". The test of a good split is what a

95
00:07:03,451 --> 00:07:07,824
revert would do. git revert is 🟡 CAUTION:
it adds one commit that applies the

96
00:07:07,864 --> 00:07:12,524
inverse change. In the picture from today's
demo, that's the third commit, undoing

97
00:07:12,564 --> 00:07:13,154
the second.

98
00:07:13,494 --> 00:07:18,423
Signatures. A signed commit carries its
signature inside the commit object, in a

99
00:07:18,463 --> 00:07:21,963
gpgsig header between the
committer line and the message.

100
00:07:22,003 --> 00:07:27,118
Because it's inside the object, it's covered
by the commit ID, and signing a commit

101
00:07:27,158 --> 00:07:31,963
that already exists means writing a new
commit. Don't confuse the two options:

102
00:07:32,003 --> 00:07:36,310
capital -S signs with a key, and
small -s adds the Signed-off-by text.

103
00:07:38,150 --> 00:07:42,545
For amend, forget the word "edit".
Picture a fork with two short prongs.

104
00:07:42,585 --> 00:07:46,980
Both prongs grow from the same parent.
One is the commit you made first.

105
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:52,031
The other is the commit amend made. The
branch label is moved from the first prong

106
00:07:52,071 --> 00:07:54,064
to the second. Nothing is erased.

107
00:07:54,404 --> 00:07:58,571
Now read the hook with that picture.
The server holds the first prong.

108
00:07:58,611 --> 00:08:03,438
You hold the second. Your branch is one
commit ahead and one commit behind at the

109
00:08:03,478 --> 00:08:07,705
same time. A pull merges the prongs.
That's why the hint is wrong here.

110
00:08:08,045 --> 00:08:11,449
The model's limit: the first
prong is not permanent.

111
00:08:11,489 --> 00:08:16,947
It's kept by reflog entries, and those
expire. So "nothing is erased" is true today

112
00:08:16,987 --> 00:08:18,338
and has a time limit.

113
00:08:20,178 --> 00:08:26,340
One parent, d4c9fab. Two children. The
upper one, 831f9ff, has the typo, and is

114
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:31,129
reachable only from the reflog
entries HEAD@{1} and main@{1}.

115
00:08:31,169 --> 00:08:37,723
The lower one, 8f6fa22, carries the branch
label. No arrow leads from one sibling to

116
00:08:37,763 --> 00:08:42,355
the other: the new commit doesn't
know the old one existed.

117
00:08:46,695 --> 00:08:50,681
A commit with a typo in its
message, and a forgotten test file.

118
00:08:50,721 --> 00:08:55,856
git commit --amend 🟡 CAUTION. Predict:
after the amend, how many commits does git

119
00:08:55,896 --> 00:08:59,306
log show, two or three, and
what is the ID of the tip?

120
00:08:59,346 --> 00:09:00,329
Say it out loud.

121
00:09:05,169 --> 00:09:09,899
Still two commits. The log shows
8f6fa22 where 831f9ff was.

122
00:09:09,939 --> 00:09:16,044
The Date: line in the summary is the
author date, carried over by the amend.

123
00:09:16,084 --> 00:09:20,005
Where is 831f9ff now? Say
it out loud. I'll wait.

124
00:09:24,845 --> 00:09:30,161
In the reflog, one entry down. git cat-file
-p 'HEAD@{1}' prints the old object:

125
00:09:30,201 --> 00:09:35,516
same parent, same author line, and a
different tree, committer time and message.

126
00:09:35,556 --> 00:09:37,659
Two commits that share a parent.

127
00:09:37,999 --> 00:09:43,169
No branch contains the old commit,
and git log --all doesn't list it.

128
00:09:43,209 --> 00:09:49,285
git fsck --no-reflogs reports it as dangling:
only the reflog still refers to it.

129
00:09:49,625 --> 00:09:53,783
Give it a name with git branch 🟢 SAFE,
and it's an ordinary commit again.

130
00:09:53,823 --> 00:09:55,451
The graph now shows the fork.

131
00:09:55,791 --> 00:10:00,751
The commit has been pushed. Then it's
amended. Predict what git status -sb says

132
00:10:00,791 --> 00:10:05,941
about main and origin/main, the label for
where the server's branch was last seen.

133
00:10:06,281 --> 00:10:11,247
[ahead 1, behind 1] is the diagnosis:
your branch has the new commit, and the

134
00:10:11,287 --> 00:10:12,873
upstream has the old one.

135
00:10:13,213 --> 00:10:18,037
git push 🟡 CAUTION is rejected as a
non-fast-forward, and the hint says to pull.

136
00:10:18,077 --> 00:10:21,990
Don't follow the hint: it would
merge two versions of one commit.

137
00:10:22,030 --> 00:10:25,578
Here are the two right moves I
promised, from the textbook.

138
00:10:25,618 --> 00:10:30,626
If others may have the published commit,
return to it with git reset --soft '@{u}',

139
00:10:30,666 --> 00:10:34,031
which is 🟡 CAUTION, and fix
the mistake in a new commit.

140
00:10:34,071 --> 00:10:38,409
If the branch is yours alone, replace
the published commit deliberately.

141
00:10:38,449 --> 00:10:39,930
That's a topic of Part 2.

142
00:10:42,770 --> 00:10:45,706
Without the option, Git
refuses with exit status 1.

143
00:10:45,746 --> 00:10:49,266
With it, the new commit and its
parent have the same tree ID.

144
00:10:52,106 --> 00:10:56,902
-s and two --trailer options. Three
trailers at the end of the message.

145
00:10:57,242 --> 00:11:02,213
Three ways to read them back, and one to
count by them: git interpret-trailers

146
00:11:02,253 --> 00:11:07,479
--parse, the %(trailers) format with a key,
and git shortlog grouped by a trailer.

147
00:11:07,819 --> 00:11:11,965
Now the rules, as five cases.
Try it on paper, thirty seconds.

148
00:11:12,005 --> 00:11:16,961
One: the trailer alone, after an empty
line. Two: no empty line before it.

149
00:11:17,001 --> 00:11:22,631
Three: a sentence in the same block. Four:
that sentence again, with Refs configured

150
00:11:22,671 --> 00:11:27,964
as a key. Five: more text after the block.
For each, will Git find the trailer?

151
00:11:28,004 --> 00:11:30,125
Pause me, and write your answer.

152
00:11:34,965 --> 00:11:39,135
In order: recognised. No empty
line before it: not recognised.

153
00:11:39,175 --> 00:11:44,840
A sentence inside the block: not recognised.
The same with Refs configured as a key:

154
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:49,323
recognised. Text after the block:
not recognised. Two out of five.

155
00:11:49,363 --> 00:11:52,651
If you got case four wrong,
almost everyone does.

156
00:11:55,491 --> 00:11:59,048
A message that states the problem and
why this solution, with trailers.

157
00:11:59,388 --> 00:12:02,643
The title appears wherever Git
needs one line for a commit.

158
00:12:02,683 --> 00:12:07,055
Compare it with the title of the commit
above it, "fix stuff", in each of these

159
00:12:07,095 --> 00:12:07,557
listings.

160
00:12:07,897 --> 00:12:10,263
And in a mail subject
and a patch file name.

161
00:12:13,103 --> 00:12:18,021
Two unrelated edits in the working tree, a
bug fix and a dependency bump, committed

162
00:12:18,061 --> 00:12:21,546
separately. Then the new
dependency breaks the nightly run.

163
00:12:21,586 --> 00:12:25,548
git revert 🟡 CAUTION. Predict what
is left after reverting the tip.

164
00:12:25,888 --> 00:12:29,872
The dependency bump was undone, and the
bug fix stayed, because they were two

165
00:12:29,912 --> 00:12:30,290
commits.

166
00:12:33,130 --> 00:12:37,925
The gpgsig header sits between the committer
line and the message, and continuation

167
00:12:37,965 --> 00:12:42,760
lines begin with a space. It's inside the
object. Keys, verification and trust come

168
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:43,925
later in the course.

169
00:12:45,765 --> 00:12:47,413
Five mistakes to watch for.

170
00:12:47,753 --> 00:12:52,161
git push rejected after an amend, then
a pull. Root cause: the amend created a

171
00:12:52,201 --> 00:12:56,496
sibling of the published commit; the
pull merges two versions of one commit.

172
00:12:56,836 --> 00:13:02,603
An amend swallowed unrelated staged changes.
Root cause: everything staged goes into

173
00:13:02,643 --> 00:13:06,957
an amend unless you say --only;
run git status before amending.

174
00:13:07,297 --> 00:13:11,912
A line of the message is missing. Root
cause: the line began with # and the message

175
00:13:11,952 --> 00:13:14,997
went through the editor, where
such lines are comments.

176
00:13:15,337 --> 00:13:20,853
git log --oneline shows a very long title.
Root cause: there is no empty line after

177
00:13:20,893 --> 00:13:25,472
the first line, and the title is the
text up to the first empty line.

178
00:13:25,812 --> 00:13:31,333
A trailer is not found by tooling. Root
cause: it is not in the last paragraph, or a

179
00:13:31,373 --> 00:13:35,835
sentence shares its block; add
trailers with --trailer, not by hand.

180
00:13:37,675 --> 00:13:41,817
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation
team puts the ticket number and the

181
00:13:41,857 --> 00:13:46,538
evaluation-run ID of each change into
trailers. The textbook's reason: a ticket

182
00:13:46,578 --> 00:13:51,258
number or a run ID in a trailer travels
with the commit into every clone, and a

183
00:13:51,298 --> 00:13:56,038
pull-request label doesn't. A year later,
"which commits belong to this ticket?"

184
00:13:56,078 --> 00:13:59,624
is one git log format away, on
any machine, with no network.

185
00:13:59,964 --> 00:14:04,714
And the atomic split pays off on the night
the new tokenizer release breaks the

186
00:14:04,754 --> 00:14:08,534
nightly run: one revert undoes
the bump and leaves the bug fix.

187
00:14:08,574 --> 00:14:13,507
The same separation lets you cherry-pick
the fix to a release branch, and lets git

188
00:14:13,547 --> 00:14:18,237
bisect stop on a commit small enough to
read. Both tools have their own videos

189
00:14:18,277 --> 00:14:18,601
later.

190
00:14:20,441 --> 00:14:25,845
Your turn. Do Lab 3.1, "Amend a
commit and find the old one", in

191
00:14:25,885 --> 00:14:28,056
lab-manual/m03-commits.md.

192
00:14:28,396 --> 00:14:33,670
Before the amend, write down the ID of
the tip and predict three things: whether

193
00:14:33,710 --> 00:14:38,585
that ID will still be printed by git
log, by git reflog, and by git branch

194
00:14:38,625 --> 00:14:40,445
--contains. Then check each.

195
00:14:42,285 --> 00:14:48,099
Q33: "After git commit --amend, where is
the old commit, how long does it stay, and

196
00:14:48,139 --> 00:14:49,792
how do you get it back?"

197
00:14:52,132 --> 00:14:56,919
Answer out loud. A strong answer says what
still refers to the old commit and what

198
00:14:56,959 --> 00:15:01,569
no longer does, gives the default retention
periods and what removes the object

199
00:15:01,609 --> 00:15:06,455
afterwards, and names the one-line recovery.
Add what changes if the old commit had

200
00:15:06,495 --> 00:15:07,161
been pushed.

201
00:15:09,001 --> 00:15:13,980
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say, in your own words: git commit

202
00:15:14,020 --> 00:15:19,324
--amend creates a new commit with the same
parent. The old one stays in the object

203
00:15:19,364 --> 00:15:23,691
database, reachable only from the
reflog, until its entries expire.

204
00:15:23,731 --> 00:15:28,905
If the old commit was pushed, my branch
is one ahead and one behind, and I don't

205
00:15:28,945 --> 00:15:30,013
pull to fix that.

206
00:15:30,353 --> 00:15:35,280
A trailer is a Key: value line in the last
paragraph of the message, and Git parses

207
00:15:35,320 --> 00:15:40,008
it only if the block follows the rules.
The title is everything up to the first

208
00:15:40,048 --> 00:15:45,035
